[Music]  as a part of our black history month  coverage cbs this morning is taking a  look at the challenges black americans  face  when trying to find a job or climb the  corporate ladder  research from 2019 found black americans  only made up  3.2 percent of senior leadership roles  at large companies  and according to a new linkedin survey  81 percent of black professionals say  seeing other black people in positions  of leadership  would make their workplace feel more  inclusive we spoke with zachary a  consultant and shanique a marriage and  family therapist  about what they say their experiences  have been with racial inequity at work  it seems like it's a little bit  difficult for people to either remember  my name or pronounce it correctly or  remember to pronounce it correctly  and so they would often ask for well  what's your middle name  um what's your you know do you have a  nickname which definitely makes me feel  ashamed of my name at times  because it seems like i'm not good  enough or i'm  stereotyped as a young black man in  corporate america folks have said all  types of things about me i've been told  i'm  too arrogant too aggressive sometimes  not aggressive enough  too proactive not proactive enough  looking for a job  in the corporate world i had two resumes  both resumes were the same except one  resume had shanique on there the other  resume had charlie  if i put about 10 resumes out  about seven came back for charlie  seven phone calls to offer a job or  offer an interview came back for charlie  about two came back for shanique i was  gunning for a promotion  and i was doing my thing now look i'm  killing it right like i'm actually  doing all types of work working in and  outside of my  business unit i am doing above and  beyond  i find out i got a white counterpart he  quits  close to the top of the year and then he  comes back  right before the promotion decisions are  determined and that guy  got promoted with me so equal outcomes  but  effort was not equal but what i also  realized is  i'm not the only person out there  experiencing this why create a living  corporate a digital media network  that centers and amplifies black and  brown folks at work gestures are cool  days off are cool but the reality is if  you're not letting  black and brown folks make actual  decisions that can impact  systemic change you're not doing  anything  linkedin career expert andrew mccaskill  joins us now with more on race and  and equity uh in the workplace it's all  part of our partnership with linkedin  andrew good morning to you  i want to get to the resume issue  because it's really fascinating and  disturbing  but i want to start with your reporting  so you also spoke with black  professionals about what they face in  the workplace  what'd you learn and what needs to  change  a lot needs to change what we learned  was that black professionals  overwhelmingly  saw that responded that things that were  barriers to their advancement were  not seeing people who look like them at  top in senior leadership  not having access to mentorship and  sponsorship  not feeling a sense of belonging in the  workplace and  finally just feeling like that there was  no path for them  for advancement all of those things  culminate to  to black professionals feeling like that  there's more opportunity outside of the  workforce and  working for themselves then working for  companies and corporations  so let's talk about this resume issue we  heard uh shanique say that when she  sends out her resume with the name  charlie on top of it charlie wright she  gets seven callbacks if she sends out  the same  resume with shanique right on it she  only gets two and that actually matches  up with your findings and with other  people's research  what the heck is going on there what  happens is  oftentimes unconscious bias is is in  place sometimes just actual biases in  place  but people with equal qualifications and  skills don't always get equal outcomes  and the unfortunate fact is that a lot  of professionals are starting to say  feel like that they have to whiten their  resume or remove  racial descriptors from their resumes i  went to a historically black college i  went to morehouse college i can't  imagine having to  thinking i have to take that off my  resume but the onus is on  the corporations to put things in place  to  make sure that racial discrimination for  in their  hiring process doesn't creep in and you  have to be intentional about it  it doesn't just happen organically yeah  so let's let's talk about the intentions  because i love  zachary and i love shanique i thought  they raised really great points  so what should employers do to put that  in place and be intentional give us two  top things they could do  today today is they can do blind  recruitment where  they look in at the in the hiring  process and start to just only look at  qualifications and skills  the other thing that they can do is that  they have to be  hold themselves accountable they need to  start to measure and think about  the number of people from  underrepresented groups who are applying  for roles and opportunities  and then juxtapose that to the number of  folks who actually get call backs  and get hired in the end this is not the  responsibility of the job seeker  it requires extra effort for inclusion  and that has to be something that  everybody at the corporation  is willing to to go along with it  requires extra effort  you know they also talk about fear of  retaliation uh  black people in the workplace when they  speak up about it  so you know you're looked at as  complaining or  causing trouble what other obstacles do  they face  and how to navigate that  there there's so many other obstacles  that they face but i think gail that  one in four black respondents said that  they fear retaliation  and so that means that we have to have  allies in this process  is that if you are a leader you should  be talking about inclusion  because that sets the tone for culture  people are not  leaving they're just not leaving their  jobs they're leaving toxic cultures  and they're leaving the the they're not  wanting to have to deal with things like  microaggressions at work or feeling like  there's hopelessness at work so one  thing that people can do is if you're a  leader  talk about this have the hard  conversations  it shouldn't just be black people and  people of color or people who are other  talking about inequities at work because  one in four black folks said that they  felt like  that they would receive retaliation when  they talk about inequities so we need  our non-black counterparts to jump in  and talk about it too  yeah it may be a hard conversation but  it's a necessary conversation drew  mccaskill good to see you thank you so  much for joining us today

